Hello teaching team! You've taught the last section of the very first =
Probability for AI. =F0=9F=8E=89

A few notes:

Leaderboards: the Covers =
and Section ratings tabs on the leaderboards (https://pai.stanford.=
edu/pai1/leaderboards) show how the whole team did.

Your certificate: your progress is on the "Your certificate" card on your =
PAI Home page: sections taught, shadow reviews and trainings. If something =
looks off, post in the report forum (https://pai.stanford.edu/pai1/report).

End of course survey: if you haven't yet, please fill out the section =
leader end of course survey (https://pai.stanford.edu/pai1/survey/sl-end-of=
-course). It takes about 5 minutes, and your answers will shape the next =
PAI.

Keep learning: the Keep learning page (https://pai.stanford.=
edu/pai1/keeplearning) is now live for your students, including a shelf of =
section leaders' own work.

Thank you

About 500 of you volunteered to =
teach probability to about 10,600 students around the world, one of the =
biggest math teaching teams ever assembled. Every week you learned a new =
section, prepared it and taught it live, often to students meeting these =
ideas for the first time. Together you held 2,643 sections over six weeks. =
You looked out for each other too. When a leader couldn't make it, someone =
stepped in: more than 200 covers, taught by more than 110 of you, so =
students kept their section. Here in the Lounge you answered each other's =
questions, shared your slides and tricks, and caught mistakes in our notes.=
 Your students noticed. They left more than 10,000 ratings, and in the =
final week they rated sections 4.71, the best of the course.

Thank you, all of you. It has been a really special experience. If we do =
this again, it would be wonderful to teach with you again.

All the best,
Chris

